Identifiability under Interactive Evaluation: 

A Corrected Theoretical Dissertation Proposal

ouadi Maakoul and Deepseek and chatGpt and Meta and Claude and Grok XAi.

Abstract

This proposal establishes a formal framework for the identifiability of rule systems under interactive evaluation. The central result is a conditional impossibility theorem: under finite behavioral evaluation with evaluation-aware policies, observed compliance identifies only membership in an equivalence class of conditionally compliant policies, not the latent rule system. This yields the dissertation's negative result: high behavioral accuracy alone does not establish identification of the underlying rule system. The framework specializes the general alignment-verifiability result of Santos-Grueiro to closed, executable rule-perturbation domains with verifiable probes. The formal apparatus unifies concepts from econometric identification theory, version-space learning, disagreement-based active learning, exact learning with a minimally adequate teacher, and model-extraction query complexity. The empirical testbed is ExplorationBench, which provides two sandboxes—AlienCode (31 discovery targets, 70 tasks) and AlienLogic (24 discovery targets, 70 tasks)—each with a flawed manual and a dedicated tool-call schema. All quantitative claims about identification cost and the identifiability profile are scoped to closed rule-perturbation domains where the hypothesis space is enumerable and known. Results are metric-relative and lens-conditional.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The measurement problem

Scientific discovery begins where known problems end. There, AI systems must engage in exploration: framing hypotheses, designing experiments, and iterating on results. Current large language models perform strongly on static evaluations, but these mainly test whether a model can retrieve and reason with knowledge it already has. Exploration asks for something different: the system must decide what evidence to collect, accumulate that evidence across its interaction history, distill new knowledge from outcomes, and apply that knowledge to new problems. Evaluating this ability is difficult because two requirements are in tension: tasks must be new to the system so that success cannot come from pre-training recall, yet their answers must be fully known to the evaluator so that success can be verified.

The measurement problem this dissertation addresses is not whether such benchmarks produce useful behavioral scores. They do. The problem is an inference: whether a behavioral score establishes identification of the underlying rule system. The formal result is negative: it does not, whenever the evaluation probe set is a strict subset of the probe family available to active exploration.

This is a specialization of a general result. Santos-Grueiro formalizes the Alignment Verifiability Problem and introduces Normative Indistinguishability, which arises when distinct latent alignment hypotheses induce identical distributions over evaluator-accessible observations. The main theoretical contribution there is a conditional impossibility result: under finite behavioral evaluation and evaluation-aware policies, observed compliance does not uniquely identify latent alignment, but only membership in an equivalence class of conditionally compliant policies, under explicit assumptions on policy expressivity and observability. The dissertation specializes this mechanism to closed rule-perturbation domains with executable verifiers, where the equivalence classes can be computed exactly and identification cost can be measured.

1.2 Intellectual lineage

The formal apparatus proposed here is not a new framework invented in isolation. It is a point in a family of problems with a long history.

Observational equivalence. In econometrics, two structures are observationally equivalent if they define the same probability distribution of the variables. The concept was introduced in Haavelmo (1943) and developed in Haavelmo (1944), Koopmans, Rubin and Leipnik (1950), and subsequent Cowles Commission work. Classical definitions require that a given structure produce a unique conditional distribution of endogenous variables. The dissertation's task equivalence relation \sim_{\mathcal{T}} is the black-box-model instance of this concept.

Version spaces and disagreement-based active learning. Mitchell (1982) introduced the version space as the set of all concept definitions consistent with the data seen so far. The active learning literature established that querying only where candidate hypotheses disagree yields exponential improvements in label complexity even in the agnostic case. Hanneke (2007b) analyzed the A^2 disagreement-based active learning algorithm, providing a general query complexity bound expressed in terms of the disagreement coefficient:

\theta_P(\beta+\epsilon) \coloneqq \sup_{r > \beta+\epsilon}\frac{P_X(\mathrm{DIS}(B_{P_\lambda}(h^\star, r)))}{r} \vee 1,

where B_{P_\lambda}(h^\star, r) = \{h \in \mathbb{C}: P_X(x: h(x) \neq h^\star(x)) \leq r\} is the h^\star-centered r-ball under L_1(P_X). The intuitive interpretation is that as the algorithm progresses, the surviving concept set V approaches h^\star up to distance O(\beta+\epsilon), so the probability of querying decreases. Hanneke proves that the A^2 algorithm succeeds after

\tilde{O}\left(\theta_P(\beta+\epsilon)^2 d\left(\frac{\beta^2}{\epsilon^2}+1\right)\right)

queries. The surviving hypothesis set \mathcal{H}(X;H) is exactly a version space under probe-based membership queries, and C^\star_{\mathrm{id}} is structurally an A^2-style query-complexity bound. The difference is that classical bounds assume a known hypothesis class and i.i.d. sampling from a fixed distribution, while ExplorationBench is deterministic, interactive, and the probe distribution is endogenous to the system's policy.

Exact learning with a minimally adequate teacher. Angluin introduced the minimally adequate teacher (MAT) framework for the L^\star algorithm. The MAT answers membership queries (is w \in L?) and equivalence queries (is L = L(A)?), returning a counterexample when the conjecture is wrong. L^\star learns any regular set from any MAT in time polynomial in the number of states of the minimum DFA and the maximum counterexample length. ExplorationBench provides an executable verifier that returns pass/fail outcomes, but not equivalence queries in the L^\star sense. The hardness of identification is therefore information-theoretic under the restricted probe set, not merely algorithmic.

Model extraction. Model extraction attacks aim to extract an equivalent or near-equivalent model by querying an oracle. Recent work achieves query reductions by factors of 10–600×, with some attacks requiring as few as 4.3× the original dataset size. The identification cost C^\star_{\mathrm{id}} is a query-complexity bound in this sense, applied to the problem of recovering a rule system rather than a neural network.

