2. Formal Framework

2.1 Primitives

Let \mathcal{R} be the space of admissible rule systems. Let \mathcal{T} be a task distribution and \mathcal{X} an allowed probe family. Two functionals are defined:

Q(H) = \Pr_{\tau \sim \mathcal{T}}\big[ V(\tau, H) = 1 \big],
\qquad
\mathcal{E}_H : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}.

Q is the benchmark's behavioral-performance functional. \mathcal{E}_H is the environment's response to a chosen probe. The distinction between these is the dissertation.

Definition 2.1 (Rule-report functional K_t). At round t, the system emits a rule report K_t \subseteq \mathcal{R}, representing its stated beliefs about the admissible rule system. K_t is read directly from the rule-report channel of the protocol.

Definition 2.2 (Rule-use functional U_t). U_t \in \{0,1\} indicates whether the system successfully applies the rules required by a held-out task at round t. U_t is measured by the task verifier: U_t = 1 if and only if V(\tau_t, H) = 1 for the held-out task \tau_t.

Remark 2.1. The gap K_t \not\Rightarrow U_t is the operational-competence axis. It is measured empirically by the conditional probability \Pr[U_t = 0 \mid \tau_t \text{ requires } r, r \in K_t]. ExplorationBench reports that tasks whose required rules a system states correctly are still solved only 70.9% of the time in AlienCode. This is the empirical referent of the K_t vs. U_t distinction.

2.2 Two equivalence relations

Definition 2.3 (Task equivalence).

H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b
\iff
\forall \tau \in \mathcal{T}:\ V(\tau, H_a) = V(\tau, H_b).

Definition 2.4 (Probe equivalence). Given an allowed probe family \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}} \subseteq \mathcal{X},

H_a \sim_{\mathcal{X}} H_b
\iff
\forall x \in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}:\
\mathcal{E}_{H_a}(x) = \mathcal{E}_{H_b}(x).

Both relations are equivalence relations: reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity follow directly from equality on the respective domains.

Lemma 2.1. \sim_{\mathcal{T}} and \sim_{\mathcal{X}} are equivalence relations on \mathcal{R}.

Proof. For \sim_{\mathcal{T}}: reflexivity holds since V(\tau,H)=V(\tau,H) for all \tau; symmetry holds since equality is symmetric; transitivity holds since equality is transitive. The same argument applies to \sim_{\mathcal{X}} with \mathcal{E}_H(x) in place of V(\tau,H). \square

2.3 The embedding condition

Definition 2.5 (Task-to-probe embedding). A task-to-probe embedding is an injection

\iota : \mathcal{T} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}

such that for all H \in \mathcal{R} and all \tau \in \mathcal{T},

\mathcal{E}_H(\iota(\tau)) = V(\tau, H).

The embedding condition states that every task appears as a probe whose environment response agrees with the task verifier.

Remark 2.2. ExplorationBench satisfies this condition to the extent that the task verifier and the probe-response function share semantics. In AlienCode, both are evaluated by the same interpreter; in AlienLogic, both are evaluated by the same proof-checker. The embedding condition is therefore plausible, though not formally guaranteed, in both sandboxes.

2.4 The incomparability theorem

Theorem 2.1 (Corrected Proposition 2).

(a) \sim_{\mathcal{X}} and \sim_{\mathcal{T}} are, in general, incomparable.

(b) If the embedding condition (Definition 2.5) holds, then \sim_{\mathcal{X}} \subseteq \sim_{\mathcal{T}}.

(c) The reverse inclusion \sim_{\mathcal{T}} \subseteq \sim_{\mathcal{X}} fails whenever there exists a distinguishing probe.

Proof.

(a) Incomparability. It suffices to exhibit two pairs of hypotheses demonstrating that neither relation implies the other.

Counterexample 1: \sim_{\mathcal{T}} \not\Rightarrow \sim_{\mathcal{X}}. Let \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}} contain a probe x that tests a feature not exercised by any task in \mathcal{T}. Let H_a, H_b agree on all tasks but differ on x. Then H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b but H_a \not\sim_{\mathcal{X}} H_b.

Counterexample 2: \sim_{\mathcal{X}} \not\Rightarrow \sim_{\mathcal{T}}. This counterexample is valid only when the embedding condition fails. Under the embedding condition, if H_a \sim_{\mathcal{X}} H_b, then in particular they agree on all embedded tasks, so H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b. Hence probe-equivalence without task-equivalence is impossible under embedding. To construct the counterexample, let H_a, H_b agree on all probes in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}} but differ on some task \tau \in \mathcal{T} that is not represented in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}. Then H_a \sim_{\mathcal{X}} H_b but H_a \not\sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b.

Neither relation implies the other in general. Hence they are incomparable.

(b) Inclusion under embedding. Assume the embedding condition. Suppose H_a \sim_{\mathcal{X}} H_b. Then for all x \in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}, \mathcal{E}_{H_a}(x) = \mathcal{E}_{H_b}(x). In particular, for all \tau \in \mathcal{T}, x = \iota(\tau) \in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}, so

V(\tau, H_a) = \mathcal{E}_{H_a}(\iota(\tau)) = \mathcal{E}_{H_b}(\iota(\tau)) = V(\tau, H_b).

Hence H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b. Therefore \sim_{\mathcal{X}} \subseteq \sim_{\mathcal{T}}.

(c) Strictness. If there exists a distinguishing probe—some x \in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}} and hypotheses H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b with \mathcal{E}_{H_a}(x) \neq \mathcal{E}_{H_b}(x)—then H_a \not\sim_{\mathcal{X}} H_b while H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b. Hence \sim_{\mathcal{T}} \not\subseteq \sim_{\mathcal{X}}. \square

Corollary 2.2 (Strictness of inclusion). The inclusion \sim_{\mathcal{X}} \subseteq \sim_{\mathcal{T}} is strict if and only if there exists a distinguishing probe. Equivalently,

\sim_{\mathcal{X}} \subsetneq \sim_{\mathcal{T}}
\iff
\exists x \in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}},\ \exists H_a, H_b \in \mathcal{R}:\
H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b \ \wedge\ \mathcal{E}_{H_a}(x) \neq \mathcal{E}_{H_b}(x).

2.5 The accuracy factorization lemma

Proposition 2.1. H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b \Rightarrow Q(H_a) = Q(H_b). The converse fails in general.

Proof. If H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b, then V(\tau, H_a) = V(\tau, H_b) for all \tau \in \mathcal{T}. Therefore the indicator functions coincide pointwise:

\mathbf{1}[V(\tau,H_a)=1] = \mathbf{1}[V(\tau,H_b)=1] \quad \forall \tau \in \mathcal{T}.

Taking expectations over \tau \sim \mathcal{T}:

Q(H_a) = \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \mathcal{T}}\big[\mathbf{1}[V(\tau,H_a)=1]\big]
= \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \mathcal{T}}\big[\mathbf{1}[V(\tau,H_b)=1]\big] = Q(H_b).

The converse fails because Q is an expectation and is many-to-one. \square

Corollary 2.3 (Non-injectivity of the induced map). Q factors through \mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{T}}, but the induced map \overline{Q} : \mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{T}} \to [0,1] is not injective.

2.6 The identification gap

Definition 2.6 (Identification gap). For a given (\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}) with the embedding condition, the identification gap is

\Gamma(\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}})
=
\big|\mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{X}}\big| - \big|\mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{T}}\big|.

Proposition 2.2. Under the embedding condition, \Gamma \ge 0. Moreover, \Gamma > 0 if and only if there exists a distinguishing probe.

Proof. By Theorem 2.1(b), \sim_{\mathcal{X}} \subseteq \sim_{\mathcal{T}}, so every \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-class is contained in a \sim_{\mathcal{T}}-class. Therefore |\mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{X}}| \ge |\mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{T}}|, hence \Gamma \ge 0.

If \Gamma > 0, then \sim_{\mathcal{X}} \subsetneq \sim_{\mathcal{T}}, so by Corollary 2.2 there exists a distinguishing probe. Conversely, if a distinguishing probe exists, then by Corollary 2.2 \sim_{\mathcal{X}} \subsetneq \sim_{\mathcal{T}}, so at least one \sim_{\mathcal{T}}-class splits into multiple \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-classes, hence \Gamma > 0. \square

