3. Identification Sets and Identification Cost

Definition 3.1 (Surviving hypothesis set). For a probe set X \subseteq \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}} and true rule H,

\mathcal{H}(X; H) = \big\{ H' \in \mathcal{R} :\ \forall x \in X,\ \mathcal{E}_{H'}(x) = \mathcal{E}_H(x) \big\}.

Lemma 3.1 (Monotonicity of surviving sets). If X_1 \subseteq X_2 \subseteq \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}, then \mathcal{H}(X_2; H) \subseteq \mathcal{H}(X_1; H).

Proof. If H' \in \mathcal{H}(X_2; H), then \mathcal{E}_{H'}(x) = \mathcal{E}_H(x) for all x \in X_2. Since X_1 \subseteq X_2, this holds in particular for all x \in X_1. Hence H' \in \mathcal{H}(X_1; H). \square

Lemma 3.2 (Convergence to equivalence class). \mathcal{H}(\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}; H) = [H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}}.

Proof. By definition, [H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}} = \{ H' : \forall x \in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}},\ \mathcal{E}_{H'}(x) = \mathcal{E}_H(x)\}. This is exactly \mathcal{H}(\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}; H). \square

Definition 3.2 (Identification up to an equivalence relation). X identifies H up to \sim if \mathcal{H}(X; H) \subseteq [H]_{\sim}.

Proposition 3.1 (Identification hierarchy). The following hierarchy holds:

\text{exact identification}
\;\Rightarrow\;
\sim_{\mathcal{X}}\text{-identification}
\;\Rightarrow\;
\sim_{\mathcal{T}}\text{-identification}.

The hierarchy is strict precisely when \sim_{\mathcal{X}} is strictly finer than \sim_{\mathcal{T}}.

Proof. If \mathcal{H}(X;H) \subseteq \{H\}, then since \{H\} \subseteq [H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}}, exact identification implies \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-identification. Similarly, if \mathcal{H}(X;H) \subseteq [H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}}, then since [H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}} \subseteq [H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{T}}} by Theorem 2.1(b), \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-identification implies \sim_{\mathcal{T}}-identification.

For strictness: if \sim_{\mathcal{X}} \subsetneq \sim_{\mathcal{T}}, then there exist H_a, H_b with H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b but H_a \not\sim_{\mathcal{X}} H_b. Then [H_a]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}} \subsetneq [H_a]_{\sim_{\mathcal{T}}}, so a probe set achieving \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-identification does not achieve \sim_{\mathcal{T}}-identification by the same probe set. \square

Definition 3.3 (Identification cost). Let C(X) be the cost of probe set X (a monotone functional of tool calls, probe units, and exploration tokens). The identification cost of H to tolerance \epsilon is

C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}}(H; \epsilon)
=
\min_{X \subseteq \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}}
\Big\{ C(X) :\ \operatorname{diam}\big( \mathcal{H}(X; H) \big) \le \epsilon \Big\},

with the convention that C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}} = \infty if no probe set achieves diameter \le \epsilon.

Remark 3.1 (Default metric). The metric and tolerance \epsilon are declared parameters. The natural default on \mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{T}} is the behavioral disagreement rate:

d(H_a, H_b) = \Pr_{\tau \sim \mathcal{T}}\big[ V(\tau, H_a) \neq V(\tau, H_b) \big].

This is computable given the task distribution and verifier, and it directly connects to the ExplorationBench measurement apparatus. Results are reported relative to this default unless otherwise stated.

Remark 3.2 (Scope of C^\star_{\mathrm{id}}). Computing \operatorname{diam}(\mathcal{H}(X; H)) requires the hypothesis space \mathcal{R} to be enumerable and known to the experimenter. This is tractable in AlienCode and AlienLogic, where the admissible rule systems are small, known perturbations of a fixed manual. It is not tractable as a general property of identifiability under interactive evaluation outside closed synthetic sandboxes. The dissertation's claims about C^\star_{\mathrm{id}} are therefore scoped to closed rule-perturbation domains.

Definition 3.4 (Identifiability profile). For a benchmark with task distribution \mathcal{T} and probe family \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}:

\iota(\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}})
=
\Big(
|\mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{T}}|,\
|\mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{X}}|,\
\sup_H C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}}(H; \epsilon)
\Big).

The first coordinate is the behavioral resolution of the evaluation. The second is the interactive resolution. The third is the worst-case cost of collapsing the gap between them.

