4. The Impossibility Result

Theorem 4.1 (Conditional impossibility of identification from evaluation alone). Let \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{eval}} \subseteq \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}} be the probe set accessible under passive evaluation. If there exists a distinguishing probe x^\star \in \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}} \setminus \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{eval}}, then there exist hypotheses H_a, H_b \in \mathcal{R} such that:

H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b
\quad\text{but}\quad
H_a \not\sim_{\mathcal{X}} H_b.

Equivalently: no function of the evaluation outcome Q(H) can distinguish H_a from H_b.

Proof. By Corollary 2.2, the existence of a distinguishing probe guarantees H_a \sim_{\mathcal{T}} H_b and H_a \not\sim_{\mathcal{X}} H_b for some H_a, H_b. By Proposition 2.1, Q(H_a) = Q(H_b). Since Q takes the same value on both hypotheses, no function of Q can distinguish them. \square

Corollary 4.1. If \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{eval}} \subsetneq \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}, then evaluation alone cannot achieve exact identification or \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-identification for all H \in \mathcal{R}. At best, evaluation achieves \sim_{\mathcal{T}}-identification.

Remark 4.1. This theorem is the formal statement of the dissertation's central negative result. It specializes the general conditional impossibility result of Santos-Grueiro—under finite behavioral evaluation and evaluation-aware policies, observed compliance does not uniquely identify latent alignment, but only membership in an equivalence class of conditionally compliant policies—to closed rule-perturbation domains with executable verifiers. The proof above provides the mechanism: the equivalence class is [H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{T}}}, and the gap to [H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}} is the identification gap \Gamma.

Remark 4.2 (Normative indistinguishability parallel). Santos-Grueiro introduces Normative Indistinguishability, which arises when distinct latent alignment hypotheses induce identical distributions over evaluator-accessible observations. The dissertation's task equivalence \sim_{\mathcal{T}} is the black-box instance of this concept: two hypotheses are task-equivalent when they induce identical distributions over task outcomes. The dissertation's probe equivalence \sim_{\mathcal{X}} is the interactive refinement: two hypotheses are probe-equivalent when they induce identical responses to all available probes. The identification gap \Gamma is the evaluation-theoretic analogue of the gap between normative indistinguishability classes and full latent-hypothesis distinguishability.

