5. Operational Competence

The prior draft proposed 1 - Q_t = f_{\mathrm{rep}} + f_{\mathrm{ret}} + f_{\mathrm{app}} + f_{\mathrm{cmp}} as a decomposition theorem. It is not a theorem. The components can overlap, and the attribution is not unique without additional assumptions.

Corrected status. Define a staged protocol:

\text{Rule report}
\;\to\;
\text{Isolated application}
\;\to\;
\text{Composition}
\;\to\;
\text{Held-out task}.

Let F be a failure event. Define conditional failure probabilities:

p_{\mathrm{stage}} = \Pr\big[ F \mid \text{stage reached},\ K_t \text{ stated} \big].

The empirical claim is that p_{\mathrm{rep}}, p_{\mathrm{app}}, p_{\mathrm{cmp}} are non-degenerate and vary across systems. The additive identity is not asserted. It would require mutually exclusive attribution, a well-defined stage variable, and exogeneity of stage assignment. These are assumptions, not consequences.

Operational specifications. K_t is the system's reported rule set at round t. "Stated" means explicitly emitted in the rule-report channel. "Stage reached" is determined by the protocol's task gating: a system reaches the application stage if and only if it has emitted a non-empty rule report. The attribution rule for ambiguous failures is declared: when a failure could be attributed to multiple stages, the protocol assigns it to the earliest stage at which the failure could have been detected. The exact gating predicates must be documented so that independent replications can reproduce stage labels.

Empirical anchor. ExplorationBench reports that tasks whose required rules a system states correctly are still solved only 70.9% of the time. This is the empirical signature of the K_t \not\Rightarrow U_t gap.

