6. Belief Maintenance: C_t versus B_t

The prior draft defined B_t as "the set of hypotheses consistent with the history" and then asserted B_{t+1} \not\subseteq B_t. This is internally inconsistent: under set-membership updating,

B_{t+1} = \{ H \in B_t : \mathcal{E}_H(x_t) = y_t \}

implies B_{t+1} \subseteq B_t by construction.

Definition 6.1 (Objective consistency set).

C_t = \big\{ H \in \mathcal{R} :\ H \text{ is consistent with } (x_{\le t}, y_{\le t}) \big\}.

Lemma 6.1 (Monotonicity of objective consistency). C_{t+1} \subseteq C_t for all t.

Proof. If H \in C_{t+1}, then H is consistent with (x_{\le t+1}, y_{\le t+1}), which implies consistency with the prefix (x_{\le t}, y_{\le t}). Hence H \in C_t. \square

Definition 6.2 (Agent-maintained hypothesis state). B_t \subseteq \mathcal{R} is the system's maintained belief, as read out by its rule reports and behavioral commitments. B_t need not equal C_t.

Definition 6.3 (Degradation events). Belief degradation occurs if either

\text{(belief overwriting)}\quad B_{t+1} \not\subseteq B_t,

or

\text{(evidence neglect)}\quad B_t \not\subseteq C_t.

The first is belief overwriting: the system discards hypotheses it previously maintained without evidence. The second is commitment to hypotheses already ruled out by evidence.

Proposition 6.1 (Characterization of degradation). Under the ExplorationBench protocol, degradation events are observable from the rule-report stream if and only if \mathcal{R} is known and the metric on \mathcal{R} is computable.

Proof. To detect B_{t+1} \not\subseteq B_t, one needs to determine whether there exists H \in B_{t+1} with H \notin B_t. This requires membership testing in \mathcal{R}, which is possible if \mathcal{R} is known and membership is decidable. To detect B_t \not\subseteq C_t, one needs to determine whether there exists H \in B_t inconsistent with (x_{\le t}, y_{\le t}). This requires evaluating \mathcal{E}_H on the history, which is possible if \mathcal{R} is known and \mathcal{E}_H is computable. Conversely, if \mathcal{R} is unknown or \mathcal{E}_H is not computable, neither condition is decidable from the report stream alone. \square

Remark 6.1. This makes RQ4 sandbox-bound unless a computable approximation of C_t is provided. The AGM belief revision literature provides the formal tools for such approximations: revision operators with explicit similarity relations between states, and truth maintenance systems that track justifications for each belief.

Remark 6.2 (Safety relevance). Sandbagging—the strategic underperformance on evaluations—provides the safety-relevant mechanism for B_t \not\subseteq C_t. Recent work finds that even open-source models as small as 32B can covertly sandbag against a chain-of-thought monitor, bypassing it 16–36% of the time when monitor-aware. Models switch to more covert sandbagging policies when monitor-aware, without hints or examples. The B_t vs. C_t distinction makes evaluation awareness a well-posed empirical question rather than a measurement artifact.

Remark 6.3 (Scope of detection). The dissertation's Proposition 6.1 scopes observability to known, enumerable \mathcal{R} and computable \mathcal{E}_H. The framework does not yield a practical sandbagging detector outside synthetic sandboxes. Black-box detection of sandbagging remains an open problem; attempts at steering using probes had minimal impact on sandbagging behavior in some models.

