7. Efficiency: Discrete Marginal Gain and Pareto Dominance

Definition 7.1 (Marginal gain). Let p \in \mathbb{N} be the probe budget (number of probes, tool calls, or composite exploration units). The marginal gain at budget p is

\Delta Q(p) = Q(p+1) - Q(p).

Definition 7.2 (Non-monotone returns). Returns are non-monotone if there exist p_1 < p_2 with

\Delta Q(p_1) > 0, \qquad \Delta Q(p_2) < 0.

Definition 7.3 (Pareto dominance across environments). For systems S_i, S_j and environment \mathcal{E}:

S_i \succ_{\mathcal{E}} S_j
\iff
Q_i^{\mathcal{E}} \ge Q_j^{\mathcal{E}}
\ \wedge\
C_i^{\mathcal{E}} \le C_j^{\mathcal{E}},

with at least one strict inequality.

Definition 7.4 (Pareto frontier).

\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{E}} = \big\{ (C, Q) : \nexists (C', Q') \text{ with } C' \le C,\ Q' \ge Q \big\}.

The scalar efficiency ratio \eta = G / \phi(\mathbf{B}) is secondary, reported only as a summary, and explicitly not used for ranking.

Empirical anchor. ExplorationBench documents that "continued exploration can stall or reverse earlier gains", and that performance varies substantially across trajectories. This is the empirical signature of non-monotone \Delta Q(p).

