8. Research Questions and Hypotheses

RQ1 (core). For a given (\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}), what is the identifiability profile \iota(\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}), and what probe sets achieve identification up to \sim_{\mathcal{T}} or \sim_{\mathcal{X}}?

RQ2 (extension). Given a system that reports a rule set correctly, how much of the residual error is attributable to stage-specific failure under a declared attribution rule?

RQ3 (extension). Is Pareto dominance stable across sandboxes, and how does \Delta Q(p) behave within a trajectory?

RQ4 (extension). Under what conditions does B_t \not\subseteq C_t or B_{t+1} \not\subseteq B_t, and can these be detected from the rule-report stream alone?

ID Statement Falsifier
H1 On the sandbox, \sim_{\mathcal{X}} \subsetneq \sim_{\mathcal{T}}. \sim_{\mathcal{X}} = \sim_{\mathcal{T}}
H2 C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}} varies by more than an order of magnitude across \mathcal{T}. Identification cost is uniform across tasks
H3 Stage-specific failure probabilities are non-degenerate. One stage absorbs all residual error
H4 \Delta Q(p) is non-monotone in at least one trajectory per system. \Delta Q is monotone within all trajectories
H5 Pareto dominance is not stable across sandboxes. Dominance transfers with high agreement
H6 B_t \not\subseteq C_t occurs in a non-trivial fraction of trajectories. Maintained beliefs remain consistent throughout

Each is falsifiable under the ExplorationBench protocol or a direct extension. H6 requires known \mathcal{R} and computable \mathcal{E}_H; the experimental protocol must record exactly how membership in C_t is decided.

