9. Limitations

L1 (C1 inheritance). All gap derivations are conditional on the fixed extraction lens L_0. Recurrence across papers is paper-level, not lens-level.

L2 (Determinism). The sandboxes are deterministic. Real scientific exploration is not.

L3 (Horizon). Four rounds, \le 48 tool calls. Short relative to scientific practice.

L4 (Construct validity). Two sandboxes cannot establish exploration as a single latent construct. The reported Spearman 0.35 is evidence against.

L5 (Trajectory sampling). n = 3 in the source benchmark is insufficient for P_i(M). This dissertation expands to n \ge 10. The source paper provides variance data usable for a power calculation: performance varies substantially across trajectories.

L6 (No human baseline). Absolute competence remains uncalibrated.

L7 (Identification cost metric). C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}} depends on the declared metric on \mathcal{R} and on \epsilon. Both are choices, and both must be reported alongside any result.

L8 (Enumerability of \mathcal{R}). C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}} presupposes an enumerable hypothesis space known to the experimenter. This is tractable in closed rule-perturbation domains such as AlienCode and AlienLogic, but not as a general property of identifiability under interactive evaluation. Every claim about C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}} or the identifiability profile \iota is scoped to closed rule-perturbation domains. Outside those domains, the quantities are not operational. This is the most important limitation and conditions all quantitative claims.

