10. Closing Claim

The corrected claim:

\boxed{
\text{High behavioral accuracy alone does not establish identification of the underlying rule system.}
}

This is the dissertation's negative result. It criticizes an inference, not a measurement. The formal support is Theorem 4.1: if there exists a distinguishing probe outside the evaluation probe set, then evaluation accuracy is identical across hypotheses that are genuinely distinct under interaction.

Corollary 10.1. Exploration can identify distinctions that evaluation cannot. This follows directly from Theorem 2.1(c) and Theorem 4.1: when \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{eval}} \subsetneq \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}, the probe equivalence relation is strictly finer than the task equivalence relation, and the additional distinctions are recoverable only through active exploration.

What the proposal does not claim. The impossibility mechanism is not new in the broader alignment-verifiability literature; it is a specialization of the Santos-Grueiro result to closed, executable rule-perturbation domains. The identification cost C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}} is not a general, computable property of interactive evaluation of LLMs; it is scoped to closed domains with enumerable \mathcal{R}. The framework does not yield practical detectors for sandbagging or evaluation awareness outside synthetic sandboxes.

