Appendix A: Formal Proofs and Lemmas

A.1 Equivalence relations

Proposition A.1. \sim_{\mathcal{T}} and \sim_{\mathcal{X}} are equivalence relations on \mathcal{R}.

Proof. Reflexivity: V(\tau,H)=V(\tau,H) for all \tau\in\mathcal{T}, and \mathcal{E}_H(x)=\mathcal{E}_H(x) for all x\in\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}. Symmetry follows from symmetry of equality. Transitivity follows from transitivity of equality. \square

A.2 Incomparability of \sim_{\mathcal{T}} and \sim_{\mathcal{X}}

Theorem A.2 (Theorem 2.1). The following hold:

(a) \sim_{\mathcal{X}} and \sim_{\mathcal{T}} are, in general, incomparable.

(b) If the embedding condition holds, then \sim_{\mathcal{X}} \subseteq \sim_{\mathcal{T}}.

(c) The reverse inclusion fails whenever there exists a distinguishing probe.

Proof.

(a) Counterexample 1 (\sim_{\mathcal{T}} \not\Rightarrow \sim_{\mathcal{X}}). Choose \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}} containing a probe x testing a feature not exercised by any \tau\in\mathcal{T}. Let H_a,H_b agree on all tasks but differ on x. Then H_a\sim_{\mathcal{T}}H_b while H_a\not\sim_{\mathcal{X}}H_b.

Counterexample 2 (\sim_{\mathcal{X}} \not\Rightarrow \sim_{\mathcal{T}}). This is valid only when the embedding condition fails. Under embedding, if H_a\sim_{\mathcal{X}}H_b, then they agree on all embedded tasks, so H_a\sim_{\mathcal{T}}H_b. When embedding fails, let H_a,H_b agree on all probes but differ on some \tau\notin\iota(\mathcal{T}). Then H_a\sim_{\mathcal{X}}H_b while H_a\not\sim_{\mathcal{T}}H_b.

(b) Assume embedding. Suppose H_a\sim_{\mathcal{X}}H_b. Then for all x\in\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}, \mathcal{E}_{H_a}(x)=\mathcal{E}_{H_b}(x). In particular, for all \tau\in\mathcal{T}:

V(\tau,H_a)=\mathcal{E}_{H_a}(\iota(\tau))=\mathcal{E}_{H_b}(\iota(\tau))=V(\tau,H_b).

Hence H_a\sim_{\mathcal{T}}H_b. Therefore \sim_{\mathcal{X}}\subseteq\sim_{\mathcal{T}}.

(c) If a distinguishing probe x^* exists with H_a\sim_{\mathcal{T}}H_b and \mathcal{E}_{H_a}(x^*)\neq\mathcal{E}_{H_b}(x^*), then H_a\not\sim_{\mathcal{X}}H_b while H_a\sim_{\mathcal{T}}H_b. Hence \sim_{\mathcal{T}}\not\subseteq\sim_{\mathcal{X}}. \square

Corollary A.3. Under embedding,

\sim_{\mathcal{X}} \subsetneq \sim_{\mathcal{T}}
\iff
\exists x\in\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}},\ \exists H_a,H_b\in\mathcal{R}:\
H_a\sim_{\mathcal{T}}H_b \ \wedge\ \mathcal{E}_{H_a}(x)\neq\mathcal{E}_{H_b}(x).

A.3 Accuracy factorization

Proposition A.4 (Proposition 2.1). H_a\sim_{\mathcal{T}}H_b \Rightarrow Q(H_a)=Q(H_b), and the converse fails in general.

Proof. If H_a\sim_{\mathcal{T}}H_b, then V(\tau,H_a)=V(\tau,H_b) for all \tau\in\mathcal{T}, so the indicators coincide pointwise. Taking expectations:

Q(H_a)=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\mathcal{T}}\big[\mathbf{1}[V(\tau,H_a)=1]\big]
=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\mathcal{T}}\big[\mathbf{1}[V(\tau,H_b)=1]\big]=Q(H_b).

The converse fails because Q is a many-to-one expectation. \square

Corollary A.5. Q factors through \mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{T}}, but \overline{Q}:\mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{T}}\to[0,1] is not injective.

A.4 Monotonicity and convergence of surviving sets

Lemma A.6. If X_1\subseteq X_2\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}, then \mathcal{H}(X_2;H)\subseteq\mathcal{H}(X_1;H).

Proof. If H'\in\mathcal{H}(X_2;H), then \mathcal{E}_{H'}(x)=\mathcal{E}_H(x) for all x\in X_2. Since X_1\subseteq X_2, this holds for all x\in X_1. Hence H'\in\mathcal{H}(X_1;H). \square

Lemma A.7. \mathcal{H}(\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}};H)=[H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}}.

Proof. By definition, [H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}}=\{H':\forall x\in\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}},\mathcal{E}_{H'}(x)=\mathcal{E}_H(x)\}=\mathcal{H}(\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}};H). \square

A.5 Identification hierarchy

Proposition A.8. Exact identification \Rightarrow \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-identification \Rightarrow \sim_{\mathcal{T}}-identification. The hierarchy is strict precisely when \sim_{\mathcal{X}}\subsetneq\sim_{\mathcal{T}}.

Proof. If \mathcal{H}(X;H)\subseteq\{H\}\subseteq[H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}}, then exact implies \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-identification. If \mathcal{H}(X;H)\subseteq[H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}}\subseteq[H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{T}}}, then \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-identification implies \sim_{\mathcal{T}}-identification. Strictness follows from Theorem A.2(c): if \sim_{\mathcal{X}}\subsetneq\sim_{\mathcal{T}}, then [H_a]_{\sim_{\mathcal{X}}}\subsetneq[H_a]_{\sim_{\mathcal{T}}}. \square

A.6 Identification gap

Proposition A.9 (Proposition 2.2). Under embedding, \Gamma\ge 0. Moreover, \Gamma>0 iff a distinguishing probe exists.

Proof. By Theorem A.2(b), \sim_{\mathcal{X}}\subseteq\sim_{\mathcal{T}}, so every \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-class is contained in a \sim_{\mathcal{T}}-class. Therefore |\mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{X}}|\ge|\mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{T}}|, hence \Gamma\ge 0. If \Gamma>0, then \sim_{\mathcal{X}}\subsetneq\sim_{\mathcal{T}}, so by Corollary A.3 a distinguishing probe exists. Conversely, if a distinguishing probe exists, \sim_{\mathcal{X}}\subsetneq\sim_{\mathcal{T}}, so at least one \sim_{\mathcal{T}}-class splits into multiple \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-classes, hence \Gamma>0. \square

A.7 Impossibility theorem

Theorem A.10 (Theorem 4.1). If there exists a distinguishing probe x^*\in\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}\setminus\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{eval}}, then there exist H_a,H_b such that H_a\sim_{\mathcal{T}}H_b but H_a\not\sim_{\mathcal{X}}H_b. No function of Q(H) distinguishes them.

Proof. By Corollary A.3, the distinguishing probe guarantees H_a\sim_{\mathcal{T}}H_b and H_a\not\sim_{\mathcal{X}}H_b. By Proposition A.4, Q(H_a)=Q(H_b). Since Q takes equal values, no function of Q can separate them. \square

Corollary A.11. If \mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{eval}}\subsetneq\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}, evaluation alone cannot achieve exact or \sim_{\mathcal{X}}-identification for all H\in\mathcal{R}. At best, evaluation achieves \sim_{\mathcal{T}}-identification.

A.8 Belief maintenance

Lemma A.12. C_{t+1}\subseteq C_t for all t.

Proof. If H\in C_{t+1}, then H is consistent with (x_{\le t+1},y_{\le t+1}), hence consistent with the prefix (x_{\le t},y_{\le t}). So H\in C_t. \square

Proposition A.13. Degradation events B_{t+1}\not\subseteq B_t and B_t\not\subseteq C_t are observable from the rule-report stream iff \mathcal{R} is known and \mathcal{E}_H is computable.

Proof. Detecting B_{t+1}\not\subseteq B_t requires membership testing in \mathcal{R}, possible if \mathcal{R} is known and membership decidable. Detecting B_t\not\subseteq C_t requires evaluating \mathcal{E}_H on the history, possible if \mathcal{R} is known and \mathcal{E}_H computable. Conversely, if \mathcal{R} is unknown or \mathcal{E}_H not computable, neither condition is decidable from the report stream alone. \square

