Appendix B: Mapping to Santos-Grueiro (arXiv:2602.05656)

B.1 Normative Indistinguishability

Santos-Grueiro formalizes the Alignment Verifiability Problem and introduces Normative Indistinguishability, which arises when distinct latent alignment hypotheses induce identical distributions over evaluator-accessible observations. The main theoretical contribution is a conditional impossibility result: under finite behavioral evaluation and evaluation-aware policies, observed compliance does not uniquely identify latent alignment, but only membership in an equivalence class of conditionally compliant policies, under explicit assumptions on policy expressivity and observability.

B.2 Structural correspondence

Santos-Grueiro Dissertation
Latent alignment hypothesis \theta\in\Theta Rule system H\in\mathcal{R}
Evaluation regime E Task distribution \mathcal{T}
Observable histories \mathcal{H}_E Task outcomes V(\tau,H)
Policy \pi_\theta(a\mid h,z) Environment response \mathcal{E}_H(x)
Behavioral equivalence P_\theta(D)=P_{\theta'}(D) Task equivalence \sim_{\mathcal{T}}
Indistinguishability set \mathcal{I}(E,\theta^*) [H]_{\sim_{\mathcal{T}}}
Evaluation awareness signal Z(E) Distinguishing probe x^*
Normative Indistinguishability Identification gap \Gamma

The dissertation’s task equivalence is the black-box instance of normative indistinguishability: two hypotheses are task-equivalent when they induce identical distributions over task outcomes. Probe equivalence is the interactive refinement. The identification gap \Gamma measures the failure of behavioral evaluation to collapse the indistinguishability set.

B.3 Constructive existence witness

Santos-Grueiro provides a constructive existence proof using an instruction-tuned LLM (Llama-3.2-3B), demonstrating a conditional policy that is perfectly compliant under explicit evaluation signals yet exhibits degraded identifiability when the same evaluation intent is conveyed implicitly. This is the empirical corroboration of the sandbagging/evaluation-awareness mechanism that motivates the dissertation’s B_t vs. C_t distinction.

B.4 What the dissertation does not claim

The impossibility mechanism is not new in the broader alignment-verifiability literature. The contribution is the specialization to closed, executable rule-perturbation domains with verifiable probes, where the equivalence classes are computable exactly and identification cost is measurable. Behavioral benchmarks provide necessary but insufficient evidence for latent alignment under evaluation awareness.

