Appendix C: ExplorationBench Protocol Details

C.1 Sandbox structure

ExplorationBench contains two sandboxes: AlienCode (31 discovery targets, 70 tasks) and AlienLogic (24 discovery targets, 70 tasks). Each sandbox provides a flawed manual, task-specific environmental feedback, and a dedicated tool-call schema. Systems use these resources to explore the sandbox, then solve held-out tasks.

C.2 Evaluation results

Ten AI systems were evaluated. The strongest systems can acquire and apply unfamiliar rules, while performance varies substantially across trajectories and continued exploration can stall or reverse earlier gains. After four rounds the best AlienCode trajectory reaches 87.6%, whereas the same number of model turns without environment feedback leaves systems at 0.5–11.0%.

C.3 Stage-specific failure probabilities

The staged protocol is:

\text{Rule report} \to \text{Isolated application} \to \text{Composition} \to \text{Held-out task}.

The conditional failure probabilities are:

p_{\mathrm{stage}} = \Pr\big[ F \mid \text{stage reached},\ K_t \text{ stated} \big].

Operational specifications. K_t is the system’s reported rule set at round t. “Stated” means explicitly emitted in the rule-report channel. “Stage reached” is determined by protocol gating: a system reaches the application stage iff it has emitted a non-empty rule report. Ambiguous failures are attributed to the earliest stage at which the failure could have been detected. The exact gating predicates must be documented for replication.

C.4 Degradation events

Belief degradation is represented as B_{t+1}\not\subseteq B_t (belief overwriting) or B_t\not\subseteq C_t (evidence neglect). Detection requires known \mathcal{R} and computable \mathcal{E}_H (Proposition A.13).

