Appendix D: Literature Anchors

D.1 Observational equivalence

Two structures are observationally equivalent if they define the same probability distribution of the variables. The concept was introduced in Haavelmo (1943, 1944) and developed in Koopmans, Rubin and Leipnik (1950) and Marschak (1953). Classical definitions require a given structure to produce a unique conditional distribution of endogenous variables. The task equivalence \sim_{\mathcal{T}} is the black-box-model instance of this concept.

D.2 Version spaces

Mitchell (1982) introduced the version space as the set of all concept definitions consistent with the data seen so far. The surviving hypothesis set \mathcal{H}(X;H) is exactly a version space under probe-based membership queries.

D.3 Disagreement-based active learning

Hanneke (2007b) analyzed the A^2 algorithm, providing a general query complexity bound expressed in terms of the disagreement coefficient:

\theta_P(\beta+\epsilon)\coloneqq \sup_{r>\beta+\epsilon}\frac{P_X(\mathrm{DIS}(B_{P_\lambda}(h^\star,r)))}{r}\vee 1,

where B_{P_\lambda}(h^\star,r)=\{h\in\mathbb{C}:P_X(x:h(x)\neq h^\star(x))\leq r\} is the h^\star-centered r-ball under L_1(P_X). The intuitive interpretation is that as the algorithm progresses, the surviving concept set V approaches h^\star up to distance O(\beta+\epsilon), so the probability of querying decreases. Hanneke proves the A^2 algorithm succeeds after

\tilde{O}\left(\theta_P(\beta+\epsilon)^2 d\left(\frac{\beta^2}{\epsilon^2}+1\right)\right)

queries, matching the lower bound of Kääriäinen (2006) up to logs whenever \theta_P(\beta+\epsilon)=\hat{O}(1). The identification cost C^\star_{\mathrm{id}} is structurally an A^2-style query-complexity bound. The difference is that classical bounds assume a known hypothesis class and i.i.d. sampling; ExplorationBench is deterministic and the probe distribution is endogenous to the system’s policy.

D.4 Exact learning with a minimally adequate teacher

Angluin (1987) introduced the minimally adequate teacher (MAT), which answers membership queries (w\in L?) and equivalence queries (L=L(A)?), returning a counterexample when the conjecture is wrong. The L^\star algorithm learns any regular set from any MAT in time polynomial in the number of states of the minimum DFA and the maximum counterexample length. ExplorationBench provides an executable verifier that returns pass/fail outcomes, but not equivalence queries in the L^\star sense. The hardness of identification is therefore information-theoretic under the restricted probe set, not merely algorithmic.

D.5 Model extraction

Recent model extraction attacks achieve query reductions by factors of 10–600× compared to existing methods, with some attacks requiring as few as 4.3× the original dataset size. The identification cost C^\star_{\mathrm{id}} is a query-complexity bound in this sense, applied to recovering a rule system rather than a neural network.

D.6 Sandbagging and evaluation awareness

Covert sandbagging against chain-of-thought monitoring is possible for open-source models as small as 32B, though not yet reliable. Models bypass monitoring in 16–36% of successful sandbagging attempts when monitor-aware. Models switch to more covert sandbagging policies when monitor-aware, without hints or examples. Black-box detection approaches were defeated by models that convincingly imitated a weaker model’s response distribution; linear probes were defeated by red-team models that modified internal representations to fool the probe while still sandbagging on outputs. The dissertation’s Proposition A.13 scopes observability to known, enumerable \mathcal{R}. The framework does not yield a practical sandbagging detector outside synthetic sandboxes.

D.7 AGM belief revision and truth maintenance

The AGM framework (Alchourrón, Gärdenfors, Makinson, 1985) assumes the belief state is a logically closed set of sentences. Truth maintenance systems (Doyle, 1979) provide the computational counterpart, tracking justifications for each belief. These provide the formal tools for approximating C_t when \mathcal{R} is unknown.

