Appendix E: Default Metric and Tolerance

E.1 Behavioral disagreement rate

The default metric on \mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{T}} is:

d(H_a,H_b) = \Pr_{\tau\sim\mathcal{T}}\big[ V(\tau,H_a)\neq V(\tau,H_b) \big].

This is computable given the task distribution and verifier, and directly connects to the ExplorationBench measurement apparatus. Results are reported relative to this default unless otherwise stated.

E.2 Identification cost

C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}}(H;\epsilon)
=
\min_{X\subseteq\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}}}
\Big\{ C(X) :\ \operatorname{diam}\big( \mathcal{H}(X;H) \big)\leq\epsilon \Big\},

with C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}}=\infty if no probe set achieves diameter \le\epsilon. C(X) is a monotone functional of tool calls, probe units, and exploration tokens.

E.3 Identifiability profile

\iota(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{probe}})
=
\Big(
|\mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{T}}|,\
|\mathcal{R}/\!\sim_{\mathcal{X}}|,\
\sup_H C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}}(H;\epsilon)
\Big).

The first coordinate is behavioral resolution. The second is interactive resolution. The third is worst-case cost of collapsing the gap. Every claim about C^{\star}_{\mathrm{id}} or \iota is scoped to closed rule-perturbation domains where \mathcal{R} is enumerable and known.

